## Where we are

.html ../slides-common/figures.html
.html figures/three-lenses.html

: Part I found memory races. Part III will inspect a stopped program. This part answers a different question entirely: where did the time actually go?

## The runtime's flight-data recorder

The execution tracer records **every scheduling event** in your program —

goroutines starting, stopping, blocking, waiting to be scheduled, entering
syscalls, GC pauses —

with **nanosecond timestamps**, and gives you a timeline you can scrub through.

: This is the pitch in one slide. Everything else is detail.

## Why logs and CPU profiles aren't enough

.html figures/tool-coverage.html

: Logs tell you what your code chose to print. CPU profiles tell you where CPU time was spent, but nothing about the time spent NOT running.
:
: The tracer shows the gaps: scheduling latency, goroutines stuck on channels and mutexes, hidden serialization, GC stop-the-world pauses.
:
: Every exercise in this section is a bug where the logs look vague — "it's slow sometimes" — and the trace makes the cause visually obvious.

## One more reason this matters in 2026

As AI coding agents write more of our code, engineers spend more of their time
on architecture, deployment, and debugging.

An execution trace is exactly the kind of data **neither a human nor an
AI agent can get by reading source.**

A trace file — or a flight recorder snapshot from production — is concrete
evidence you can hand to an agent to reason from, instead of letting it guess.

: Same framing as Part I, cashed in by the ex4 capstone: you'll build a trace analyzer and hand it to an agent over MCP.

## What you'll learn

- How to capture traces: `trace.Start`, `go test -trace`, `net/http/pprof`, and
  the flight recorder
- How to read the trace viewer: procs, goroutine states, blocking, GC
- Goroutine analysis and the derived profiles (sync block, scheduler latency)
- Annotating your own code with tasks, regions, and logs
- Capturing rare production events with `runtime/trace.FlightRecorder`

: Five things. The middle one — reading the viewer — is the skill that transfers to every Go program they'll ever profile.

## This section

.html figures/schedule.html

: Pressure valves, in the order to pull them: compress ex2 to a guided walkthrough (-15), then ex3-io to take-home (-20, last resort — it's this section's best exercise).

## Four ways to get a trace — 1. Whole program

Bracket the code you care about:

	f, _ := os.Create("out.trace")
	trace.Start(f)
	defer trace.Stop()

: The blunt instrument. Great for a reproducible local run, useless for a service that's already running.

## 2. Tests and benchmarks

	go test -trace=trace.out ./...

The cheapest way to get a trace of a specific code path — you already have the
test that exercises it.

: Worth pausing on: most people never realize the test flag exists. It's the fastest path from "I wonder" to "here's the timeline".

## 3. Live services

	import _ "net/http/pprof"

	// then:
	curl -o trace.out 'http://localhost:8080/debug/pprof/trace?seconds=5'

If you already expose pprof, you already have this. No redeploy needed.

: Ask the room how many expose /debug/pprof in production. Most hands go up. Most of them have never pulled a trace from it.

## 4. Rare events in production — the flight recorder

Go 1.25+. Keeps the last few seconds in a ring buffer; snapshot when something
goes wrong.

	fr := trace.NewFlightRecorder(trace.FlightRecorderConfig{
	    MinAge:   5 * time.Second,
	    MaxBytes: 16 << 20,
	})
	fr.Start()
	// ... on anomaly:
	fr.WriteTo(f)

: This is the one that changes what's possible. You can't trace-on-demand a spike that happens once an hour — but you can keep a rolling buffer and dump it when your own latency check trips.
:
: That's exercise 2.

## Overhead is no longer a reason to avoid this

- Since **Go 1.21**, tracing costs roughly **1–2% CPU** for typical programs —
  down from 10–20% in older releases.
- **Go 1.22** reworked the trace format so traces stream and scale.
- The flight recorder became official API in **Go 1.25**.

If your mental model of tracing overhead came from Go 1.16, update it.

: This slide exists to kill an objection before it's raised. A lot of people carry a 2019-era number in their heads.

## Reading the trace viewer

	go tool trace <file>.trace

Starts a web server. The landing page links to:

- **View trace by proc** — the main timeline
- **Goroutine analysis** (`/goroutines`)
- **Profiles** — pprof graphs derived from the trace
- **User-defined tasks / regions** — your own annotations

Use **Chrome** for the timeline page; the viewer is Chromium-based.

: Say the Chrome thing out loud and early. Someone in the room will open it in Safari and conclude the tool is broken.

## View trace by proc — the main timeline

One row per `GOMAXPROCS` logical processor, showing which goroutine ran on it at
every instant, plus rows for goroutine/heap/thread counts and GC.

Navigate with the keyboard:

- `w` / `s` — zoom in and out
- `a` / `d` — pan left and right
- click any slice to see its stack and statistics

: Drill the keyboard shortcuts. People flail with the mouse in this viewer and give up. w/s/a/d is the whole interface.

## The one table to internalize

.html figures/goroutine-states.html

A note on that last row: Go 1.26 enabled the **Green Tea** garbage collector by
default — expect **10–40% less GC overhead** than the traces you remember.

: This is the core reading skill for the section, the same way the race report was for Part I.
:
: "Runnable" is the one to dwell on — it's the state that CPU profilers structurally cannot show you.
:
: Green Tea was an opt-in experiment in Go 1.25 and is on by default in 1.26. It improves marking and scanning of small objects through better locality and CPU scalability, and uses vector instructions on newer amd64 (Ice Lake / Zen 4 and up) for another ~10%.
:
: Why it matters here: if someone has an old trace or an old mental model, the GC rows will look meaningfully lighter now. Don't let them diagnose a 2024 GC problem from a 2026 trace.

## Goroutine analysis

`/goroutines` groups goroutines by start function, with per-group breakdowns:

- Execution time
- Block time (chan send/recv, select, sync, syscall)
- **Sched wait time** — how long it was runnable but not running
- ...and more

The front page also gives you a **count** per group. Sometimes that number *is*
the bug.

: In ex1, the front page shows sync.(*WaitGroup).Go.func1 with Count 1000. That number is the bug — before you read a single line of code.

## Profiles derived from the trace

pprof-style graphs the tracer can produce: network blocking, synchronization
blocking, syscall, and **scheduler latency**.

Also available headless — types are `net`, `sync`, `syscall`, `sched`:

	go tool trace -pprof=sync out.trace > sync.pprof
	go tool pprof -top sync.pprof

: The headless form is what you want in CI, or when you're on a box with no browser. Also what you'd feed to an agent.
:
: If you open one of these in pprof's web UI (-http), note that Go 1.26 changed the default view to the flame graph. The old graph view is still there under View -> Graph, or /ui/graph.

## Scheduler starvation, seen

**This is exercise 1**, coming up next: a service crunches 1000 analytics
batches — one goroutine each — while a `heartbeat` goroutine must tick every
10ms or the orchestrator restarts the instance.

The heartbeat needs *microseconds* of CPU. It still misses, by seconds.

.html figures/starvation.html

"Bound to ~GOMAXPROCS" is a moving target since Go 1.25: on Linux the runtime
respects the cgroup CPU limit, and it re-checks periodically.

: Set the scene before they look at the picture — the heartbeat row is the whole point of the figure and it means nothing without the setup.
:
: The heartbeat goroutine only needs microseconds of CPU, but when its timer fires it goes to the back of a run queue with ~1000 CPU-hungry goroutines ahead of it, each getting a ~10ms preemption quantum.
:
: The symptom as ops reports it: "randomly getting restarted under load". p50 is 11ms against a 10ms target — fine. p99 is 1.3s. Invisible in logs, invisible in a CPU profile, because the heartbeat isn't doing anything slowly; it isn't being run at all.
:
: The GOMAXPROCS note matters for the fix. Go 1.25 changed the default two ways: on Linux it considers the cgroup CPU bandwidth limit (the Kubernetes "CPU limit", not "CPU requests"), and on all OSes it periodically updates GOMAXPROCS if the limit or CPU count changes.
:
: So a worker pool sized once at startup from runtime.GOMAXPROCS(0) can be wrong later. Read it when you need it, or size the pool dynamically. Both behaviors switch off if GOMAXPROCS is set manually, or via GODEBUG containermaxprocs=0 / updatemaxprocs=0.
:
: Total 3.1s, Execution ~300µs, Block (select) ~1s — legitimate, it's waiting for the next tick — and Sched wait ~2.2s. Awake, ready, asking for CPU, for two-thirds of its life.
:
: The fix: don't spawn one goroutine per batch. Bound concurrency to roughly the number of processors. Goroutine count drops ~1000 to ~10.

## New in Go 1.26: monitoring for this, no trace required

.html figures/sched-metrics.html

: This is the biggest thing Go 1.26 added for this section, and it closes a real gap.
:
: Until now, "runnable" was a state you could only see by capturing a trace — which you have to decide to do, in advance, on the machine having the problem. Now the goroutine-state counts are ordinary runtime/metrics you can scrape every 15 seconds forever.
:
: Same division of labor as the goroutine leak profile and Delve in Part III: the cheap always-on signal tells you THAT something is wrong and roughly where; the expensive on-demand tool tells you WHY.
:
: Practical advice for the room: put /sched/goroutines/runnable and /sched/latencies on a dashboard, alert on them, and when they fire, THEN go get a trace — or a flight recorder snapshot, which is exercise 2.

## A habit worth forming

**Capture a trace before your fix and after. Compare them side by side.**

The before/after diff of the timeline is the most convincing artifact you can
attach to a PR —

or feed to an AI agent.

: Push this hard. It converts "I think this is faster" into evidence, and it's the same artifact that makes an agent useful instead of speculative.

## Coming in Go 1.27

Expected **August 2026** — days from now.

- **The goroutine leak profile goes GA.** A pprof profile listing goroutines
  that are blocked forever — parked on a channel or mutex nothing will ever
  touch again. New in 1.26, but gated behind a build flag; in 1.27
  `/debug/pprof/goroutineleak` is simply there. *Part III teaches it.*
- **Tracebacks carry pprof goroutine labels.** For modules on `go 1.27`+, the
  header line includes `runtime/pprof` labels. Same labels Delve groups by in
  Part III — now in every panic and stack dump.
- **`go tool trace -http=:6060` binds localhost only**, matching `go tool
  pprof`. Pass `-http=0.0.0.0:6060` if you really wanted all interfaces.
- **`runtime/trace` itself is unchanged.** The capture APIs and the flight
  recorder work exactly as taught.

: The first two are the ones worth dwelling on — both extend the "cheap signal first, expensive tool second" story this section has been building.
:
: The leak profile is the room's first exposure to it, so don't assume the name means anything — one sentence is enough here: a leaked goroutine is one that will never wake up, and this profile lists them with the stack that parked them. Part III has a full slide and uses it in exercise 1's stretch goal; resist teaching it twice.
:
: The GA also means the Part III stretch goal stops needing a special build. In 1.26 it's GOEXPERIMENT=goroutineleakprofile at build time or the endpoint 404s. If 1.27 has shipped by the time you present, say so and drop the GOEXPERIMENT step.
:
: The -http change will bite anyone who runs go tool trace on a remote box and browses to it. Worth 10 seconds.
:
: The -http change is the last of the teaching content — the wrap-up is next, then they're working.

## Wrap-up: when to reach for the tracer

- **When the logs say "it's slow sometimes"** and the CPU profile looks fine.
- **When you suspect the schedule**, not the code — latency without CPU load.
- **In production**, with the flight recorder armed and a trigger you control.

What it *can't* tell you: whether a memory access was safe (Part I), or what a
specific variable holds right now.

That's Part III.

: Same wrap shape as Part I: when to reach for it, what it can't tell you.
:
: This closes the taught material — the exercises are next, so don't say "then break" here. The Part III handoff line lands again at the end of the exercises.

## Your turn

- **Ex 1 — Scheduling.** Tail latency from too many runnable goroutines. The
  "Runnable" state, in the wild.
- **Ex 2 — Flight Recorder.** Catch a rare latency spike in a long-running
  service. You can't trace on demand what you can't predict.
- **Ex 3 — Hidden I/O.** A "parallel" pipeline that actually runs one record at
  a time. The trace makes the serialization impossible to miss.
- **Ex 4 — Trace Agent** *(bonus capstone)*. Parse traces programmatically with
  the reader API, then expose your analyzer to an AI agent as an MCP server.

You'll need **Go 1.25+** (1.26 recommended; these modules declare `go 1.26`),
**Chrome/Chromium** for the timeline view, and network access once for ex4 —
everything else runs fully offline.

: Ex4 needs network on first build (golang.org/x/exp/trace and the MCP SDK) — pre-fetch with go mod download before the workshop. It's the only network dependency in the repo. Its agent stage optionally uses Claude Code.
:
: On the version floor: the flight recorder exercise needs 1.25 at minimum, so that's the hard one. If someone is on 1.24 they can still do ex1 and ex3.
:
: Leave this slide up while they work — it's the only one they need on screen. Take the break when they're done, and pick up Part III after.

